Two threads apply wire events for the same id of a plain entity type (one SETs, one DELs). Afterwards a subscribe_diffs subscriber that replays every diff holds an id the store does not, or the reverse (seen: told insert, insert, remove while the store still has the row).

Cause: CellMap changes its data when insert/remove is called, but inside hyphae::batch the diff is queued when the batch closes. Every myko store write is inside a batch. Two threads can therefore change the data in one order and queue their diffs in the other. Queries, views and client subscriptions are built from the diffs, so they stay wrong until the row is written again.

